From 8f2b80cd8ceb0e533cc887460b3a6f8571f64047 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 19:09:43 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Add To-Do tab with user feedback inbox and derived build
 queue

A tenth admin section, in three parts.

1. FROM THE SITE -- user reports. A "Spot something wrong on this page?"
   control now sits on every guide and vehicle page, capturing the page,
   vehicle and guide automatically so a report is actionable without a
   back-and-forth. On a site whose value is torque specs, a user saying "this
   number looks wrong" is the highest-signal thing that can happen, and
   nothing in the app captured it before -- a wrong torque value throws no
   error and moves no metric, it just quietly makes someone strip a bolt.
   Reports are triaged new -> triaged -> resolved/wontfix, never deleted, and
   every status change is written to the audit log.

2. BUILD QUEUE -- the "what's left" list, computed live rather than
   remembered. Guide coverage gaps, vehicles missing the free hook guide,
   hand-typed vs sourced specs, Open Labor Project cache health, the demand
   backlog, open errors, draft content. This is the half that can never go
   stale: close a gap and the item disappears on its own. It reuses the
   existing guides/alerts logic rather than duplicating it.

3. YOUR LIST -- a small manual task table for the things no query can infer.
   Deliberately minimal; a heavyweight task system here would just become
   another doc that drifts.

Also surfaces untriaged reports as a critical alert on the Command Center,
above every computed signal.

Security: the public submit endpoint requires a session, caps message length
server-side, accepts only known kinds, and rate-limits to 5 per account per
hour counted in the database (an in-process counter would enforce nothing on
serverless). Admin triage and task routes check todo.manage before touching
the database. Analytics Admin gets read-only visibility, no triage.

Requires migrations/2026-09-18-todo-and-feedback.sql.

